<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Define a calendar-year risk set, calculate cause-specific deaths and person-years,
and compare crude rates by sex, age at period start and year.
</div>

**You need:** the Opal login from [Getting started](book-00-start.html) and
[the cause-of-death capstone](book-18-capstone.html). This chapter uses `omop.questionnaire`.

## Research question

How do recorded cause-specific mortality rates differ between 2023 and 2024?
The questionnaire data have deaths in both years. Everyone is observed from
2019 through 2025, with observation for this analysis stopping at death. Thus the
population alive at each year's start can contribute the whole year or time up
to death. In a dataset with gaps or later entry, intersect each observation
interval with the analysis year as well.

```{r book-local-server, include = FALSE}
# Outputs come from a local DataSHIELD server holding the same resources as the Opal in the text.
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
```

```{r chapter-setup, eval = FALSE}
library(dsOMOPClient)
conns <- ds.omop.login("https://localhost:8443", user = "administrator",
  password = "password", resource = "omop.questionnaire", server = "site")$conns
```

```{r chapter-setup-local-2, include = FALSE}
conns <- book_connect("questionnaire")$conns
```

## Find the causes

```{r find-causes}
catalogue <- ds.omop.concept.lookup(c(900201L, 900202L), conns = conns)$pooled
catalogue[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "domain_id")]
ds.omop.concept.prevalence("death", scope = "pooled", conns = conns)$pooled
mi <- omop_event("death", 900201L)
pneumonia <- omop_event("death", 900202L)
```

Myocardial infarction is **900201** and Pneumonia is **900202**. The inventory's
person counts are rounded down to multiples of five: 20 and 10 indicate bands
20–24 and 10–14. They are not period-specific numerators; we calculate those next.

## Define age for everyone at risk

Age at death cannot classify survivors' person-time. Use completed age at the
**start of the year**, preserving missing age where the birth date is incomplete.
Each binary cause indicator looks only within the selected year. A missing cause
is distinct from no recorded death from that cause.

```{r period-definition}
period_recipe <- function(start, days) {
  window <- omop_window(0, days - 1, anchor = start)
  omop_recipe(variables = list(
    sex = omop_sex(),
    age = omop_age_at(start),
    death_days = omop_time_between(start, "death"),
    mi = omop_variable(event = mi, reducer = omop_reduce("binary", window = window)),
    pneumonia = omop_variable(event = pneumonia, reducer = omop_reduce("binary", window = window))))
}
recipe_execute(period_recipe("2024-01-01", 366), out = "D_2024", conns = conns)
ds.omop.filter("D_2024", expr = omop_where(is.na(death_days) | death_days >= 0),
  newobj = "D_alive_2024", conns = conns)
ds.omop.mutate("D_alive_2024", potential_days = coalesce(death_days, 366),
  risk_days = ifelse(potential_days > 366, 366, potential_days),
  age_group = ifelse(is.na(age), "Missing", ifelse(age < 40, "under 40", "40 and over")),
  cause = ifelse(mi == 1, "Myocardial infarction",
    ifelse(pneumonia == 1, "Pneumonia", "Neither selected cause")),
  newobj = "D_risk_2024", conns = conns)
```

Remove people already dead before January 1. A survivor contributes 366 days in
2024; someone who dies contributes elapsed days to death, excluding the death
day. We convert days to person-years using **365.25**, a fixed unit conversion;
it does not change the actual 366-day calendar boundary.

## Look at deaths before calculating rates

```{r cause-by-sex}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_risk_2024$cause",
  "D_risk_2024$sex", useNA = "always",
  datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

F means female and M means male. In 2024 there are four female and eight male myocardial-infarction deaths, and
three pneumonia deaths in each sex. Six people of each sex survive the year.
The “Neither selected cause” row contains survivors in this dataset; in another
study it could also contain other causes. The denominators include survivors' time.

```{r cause-by-age}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_risk_2024$cause",
  "D_risk_2024$age_group", useNA = "always",
  datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

Six pneumonia deaths are under 40 at period start. Six myocardial-infarction
deaths are 40 or over and six have missing age. Keep the missing-age group in
the calculation so that these deaths and their person-time do not disappear.

## Calculate crude rates and uncertainty

The following function combines only released aggregates. Multiplying a released
mean by its valid N obtains the total for that same column. Every cause indicator
and the time column must describe the same complete roster. The exact Poisson
interval treats person-time as fixed and deaths as a Poisson count.

```{r rate-function, table.digits = 2, table.row.names = FALSE}
released_rates <- function(table, group, year) {
  days <- dsBaseClient::ds.mean(paste0(table, "$risk_days"),
    type = "combine", datasources = conns)$Global.Mean
  py <- days[1, "EstimatedMean"] * days[1, "Nvalid"] / 365.25
  do.call(rbind, lapply(c(mi = "Myocardial infarction", pneumonia = "Pneumonia"), function(cause) {
    variable <- if (cause == "Pneumonia") "pneumonia" else "mi"
    x <- dsBaseClient::ds.mean(paste0(table, "$", variable),
      type = "combine", datasources = conns)$Global.Mean
    stopifnot(x[1, "Nvalid"] == days[1, "Nvalid"], x[1, "Nmissing"] == 0,
      days[1, "Nmissing"] == 0)
    deaths <- round(x[1, "EstimatedMean"] * x[1, "Nvalid"])
    ci <- stats::poisson.test(deaths, T = py)$conf.int * 1000
    data.frame(Year = year, Group = group, Cause = cause, People = days[1, "Nvalid"],
      Deaths = deaths, Person_years = py, Rate_per_1000 = 1000 * deaths / py,
      Lower_95 = ci[1], Upper_95 = ci[2])
  }))
}
rates_2024 <- released_rates("D_risk_2024", "All", 2024)
rates_2024
```

In 2024, 30 people contribute 22.03 person-years. The myocardial-infarction
rate is 544.74 per 1,000 person-years (95% CI 281.48–951.56), and the pneumonia
rate is 272.37 (99.96–592.84). These intervals are wide because only 12 myocardial-infarction deaths and six
pneumonia deaths support the respective rate estimates.
These are **crude** rates: they do not adjust for the age distribution. The
intentionally high mortality in this small synthetic population is a teaching
example, not an estimate for a clinical population.

## Use each group's own denominator

```{r stratified-rates, table.digits = 2, table.row.names = FALSE}
sex_rates <- do.call(rbind, lapply(c("F", "M"), function(label) {
  ds.omop.filter("D_risk_2024", var = "sex", op = "==", value = label,
    newobj = paste0("D_sex_", label), conns = conns)
  released_rates(paste0("D_sex_", label), if (label == "F") "Female" else "Male", 2024)
}))
age_rates <- do.call(rbind, lapply(c("under 40", "40 and over", "Missing"), function(label) {
  object <- paste0("D_age_", match(label, c("under 40", "40 and over", "Missing")))
  ds.omop.filter("D_risk_2024", var = "age_group", op = "==", value = label,
    newobj = object, conns = conns)
  released_rates(object, label, 2024)
}))
sex_rates
age_rates
```

The male myocardial-infarction rate is 640.93 per 1,000 person-years (95% CI
276.71–1,262.89), against 418.98 (114.16–1,072.77) in females. The intervals
overlap. Under-40 pneumonia has six deaths in only 1.20 person-years, giving
5,003.42 per 1,000 person-years: a rate can exceed 1,000 because it is not a percentage. Age-specific rates use the age group's own time, including
survivors. A zero observed count has a positive upper confidence limit; it does
not establish that the underlying rate is zero. Small cells may be withheld in
other datasets; never replace a withheld result with zero.

## Exercises

**1. Repeat the analysis for 2023.** Use 365 calendar days. Does the same number
of deaths imply the same rate?

<details><summary>Solution 1</summary>

```{r earlier-period, table.digits = 2, table.row.names = FALSE}
recipe_execute(period_recipe("2023-01-01", 365), out = "D_2023", conns = conns)
ds.omop.filter("D_2023", expr = omop_where(is.na(death_days) | death_days >= 0),
  newobj = "D_alive_2023", conns = conns)
ds.omop.mutate("D_alive_2023", potential_days = coalesce(death_days, 365),
  risk_days = ifelse(potential_days > 365, 365, potential_days),
  newobj = "D_risk_2023", conns = conns)
rates_2023 <- released_rates("D_risk_2023", "All", 2023)
rbind(rates_2023, rates_2024)
```

Both years have 12 myocardial-infarction and six pneumonia deaths. The 2023 risk
set contains 48 people; the 2024 risk set contains 30 after excluding 18 earlier
deaths. In 2023, 39.93 person-years give rates of 300.49 for myocardial infarction
(95% CI 155.27–524.90) and 150.25 for pneumonia (55.14–327.02). More person-time
in 2023 gives lower rates despite equal numerators.

</details>

**2. Distinguish a risk from a rate.** What fraction of those alive at the start
of 2024 die from each cause during that year?

<details><summary>Solution 2</summary>

```{r risks}
data.frame(Cause = rates_2024$Cause,
  Deaths = rates_2024$Deaths, People_at_start = rates_2024$People,
  Percent = 100 * rates_2024$Deaths / rates_2024$People)
```

The risks are 40% for myocardial infarction and 20% for pneumonia over 2024.
Their denominator is 30 people, whereas the rate denominator is person-years.
This simple risk calculation relies on complete follow-up to death or year end.

</details>

## Recap

Restrict both deaths and person-time to the same year. Define age for survivors
as well as decedents, preserve missing-age time, and report counts, denominators,
rates and uncertainty together.

**Next:** [20. Case study: celecoxib versus diclofenac](book-20-case-gibleed.html).
